11. One Phase at a Time

Do not automatically jump from one phase to another.

At the end of a phase:

1. Test it.


2. Verify the result.


3. Report the result.


4. Identify remaining issues.


5. Wait for confirmation when appropriate.


6. Then proceed.



A phase that has unresolved critical failures is not complete.


---

12. One Meaningful Change at a Time

Within a phase, changes should remain understandable.

Avoid massive unrelated modifications.

Prefer:

> Implement Missile Rook.



Then:

> Test Missile Rook.



Then:

> Fix discovered edge case.



Then:

> Verify regression suite.



Then move forward.

This makes the project easier to debug and review.


---

13. Always Test What You Change

Never say:

> "This should work."



Test it.

For example:

If modifying pawn movement:

Test:

unmoved pawn

moved pawn

starting square

non-starting square

single move

double move

capture

King Swap interaction

promotion interaction


If modifying Jester:

Test its entire lifecycle, not just one movement.


---

14. Test Interactions, Not Just Individual Rules

Hess's complexity comes from interactions.

Therefore, do not stop at:

> "Each piece passes its movement tests."



Also test:

> "What happens when this piece interacts with that state?"



Examples:

King Swap + pawn movement

King Swap + pawn double-step

Jester + capture

Jester + sacrifice

Jester + no eligible sacrifice

Jester + retained turn

Queen + midfield

Queen + Raid

promotion + Jester

setup + King position

setup + Queen position

endgame + special movement



